.TH LETTERS 2
.SH NAME
letters, basename, join, cat_into, list_dir, read_dir, sort_strings, open_or_create, open_append \- what tools share
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "letters :: proc(args: []string, buf: []u8) -> (flags: string, rest: []string)"
.B "basename :: proc(path: string) -> string"
.B "join :: proc(dir, name: string, allocator := context.allocator) -> string"
.B "cat_into :: proc(buf: []u8, parts: ..string) -> string"
.B "list_dir :: proc(fd: int, allocator := context.allocator) -> []string"
.B "read_dir :: proc(path: string, allocator := context.allocator) -> (names: []string, ok: bool)"
.B "sort_strings :: proc(list: []string, allocator := context.allocator)"
.B "open_or_create :: proc(path: string, flags: u64, mode: u64 = 0o666) -> i64"
.B "open_append :: proc(path: string, mode: u64 = 0o666) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Letters
takes the leading
.RI \- abc
flag words off the front of
.I args
and answers their letters as one string in
.IR buf ,
with whatever follows.
A word that is
.B \-
alone, or that takes a value of its own, is not a flag word and is left
for the caller to see in
.IR rest .
A tool with only single-letter flags is then a
.B "for c in letters"
over a switch.
.PP
.I Basename
is the last element of
.IR path ,
trailing slashes ignored:
.B c
of
.BR /a/b/c/ ,
.B /
of
.BR / .
.I Join
is
.IR dir / name ,
from
.IR allocator ,
with no doubled slash; an empty
.I dir
answers
.I name
unchanged.
.I Cat_into
writes its
.I parts
into
.I buf
one after another and answers what fits, for a path built from a prefix
and a number without arithmetic on literal lengths.
.PP
.I List_dir
reads an open directory,
.IR fd ,
to its end and answers the names, each copied from
.IR allocator ,
in the server's order.
.I Read_dir
is
.I list_dir
of a named directory, opening and closing it; it answers
.B false
if
.I path
will not open.
.I Sort_strings
sorts
.I list
in place, a merge sort using one scratch slice from
.IR allocator .
.PP
.I Open_or_create
opens
.I path
for writing, truncated, or makes it if it does not exist: what
.B >
means to a shell.
.I Flags
is
.B O_WRONLY
or
.BR O_RDWR .
.I Open_append
opens
.I path
for writing at its current end, or makes it: what
.B >>
means.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Grep (1)
and most other tools take their flags this way:
.IP
.EX
flag_buf: [8]u8
letters, rest := libuser.letters(args, flag_buf[:])
args = rest
for c in transmute([]u8)letters {
	switch c {
	case 'i':
		fold = true
	case 'v':
		invert = true
	}
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/path.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bio (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR stat (2),
.IR basename (1),
.IR grep (1),
.IR ls (1),
.IR sort (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Read_dir
and
.I open_or_create
answer
.B false
or a negative descriptor on the same errors
.IR open (2)
does.
